            <ArticleTitle>Lower extremity necrotizing fasciitis: A unique initial presentation of Crohn's disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>215-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amsu.2015.05.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Crohn's disease is a disease of the bowel, typically presenting with diarrhea, weight loss, and abdominal pain. Complications such as abscesses, fistulas, and strictures may require surgical intervention. We would like to report a patient with Crohn's disease who presented for the first time with left lower extremity necrotizing fasciitis. There are very few reports of necrotizing fasciitis in Crohn's disease as the initial presentation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
